        <mxCell id="lossB" value="Node B loses at ID0&lt;br&gt;&lt;font style=&quot;font-size:9.5px&quot;&gt;sent 1, read back 0&lt;/font&gt;" style="text;html=1;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;fontSize=10.5;fontStyle=1;fontColor=#c62828;fontFamily=DejaVu Sans;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="747" y="294" width="180" height="28" as="geometry"/></mxCell>
        <mxCell id="win" value="&lt;b&gt;Node A wins with 0x0F0&lt;/b&gt; and keeps transmitting without a single corrupted bit. B and C become receivers of A's frame and retry at the next bus-idle." style="rounded=1;html=1;fillColor=#e8f5e9;strokeColor=#2e7d32;fontSize=11.5;fontColor=#2e7d32;fontFamily=DejaVu Sans;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="30" y="332" width="900" height="30" as="geometry"/></mxCell>
        <mxCell id="n0" value="▸ The rule is one line long: send recessive, read back dominant → you lost. Stop driving, switch to receive, keep the frame you are now hearing." style="text;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=middle;fontSize=11;fontColor=#37474f;fontFamily=DejaVu Sans;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="30" y="380" width="900" height="16" as="geometry"/></mxCell>
        <mxCell id="n1" value="▸ Dominant is 0, so the numerically &lt;b&gt;lowest identifier has the highest priority&lt;/b&gt; — priority is designed into the ID map at network design time." style="text;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=middle;fontSize=11;fontColor=#37474f;fontFamily=DejaVu Sans;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="30" y="402" width="900" height="16" as="geometry"/></mxCell>
        <mxCell id="n2" value="▸ &lt;b&gt;Non-destructive&lt;/b&gt;: nothing is discarded and no bandwidth is burned on the collision — unlike Ethernet CSMA/CD, which throws both frames away and backs off." style="text;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=middle;fontSize=11;fontColor=#37474f;fontFamily=DejaVu Sans;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="30" y="424" width="900" height="16" as="geometry"/></mxCell>
        <mxCell id="n3" value="▸ It only works if a bit driven at one end reaches the far end inside the same bit time — that constraint is why 1 Mbit/s caps the bus at roughly 40 m." style="text;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=middle;fontSize=11;fontColor=#37474f;fontFamily=DejaVu Sans;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="30" y="446" width="900" height="16" as="geometry"/></mxCell>
      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>
</mxfile>
